# 🔴 Level 3 – Advanced Concepts

## Key Length

Longer keys are generally harder to brute-force.

Example:

AES-128

↓

Very Secure

AES-256

↓

Even larger key space

Note: Security also depends on proper implementation, not just key length.

---

# Session Keys

Every HTTPS connection creates a temporary session key.

Advantages:

* Faster communication.
* Limits the impact if one session key is compromised.

---

# Forward Secrecy

Modern HTTPS often uses ephemeral key exchange (such as ephemeral Diffie-Hellman).

Benefit:

If your long-term private key is stolen in the future,

Old encrypted sessions remain protected.

---

# Common Beginner Mistakes

❌ Thinking RSA encrypts all website traffic.

❌ Believing longer keys automatically solve every security problem.

❌ Using DES in new applications.

❌ Confusing encryption with hashing.

